Replication lag creates one specific user-visible bug that appears in every application using read replicas: a user writes data and immediately reads it back from a replica that hasn't received the write yet. The profile update example is classic — user changes their name, page refreshes, old name appears.

The pattern that fixes this is **read-your-writes consistency**. After a write, subsequent reads from the same user are routed to the primary (or a replica known to be up-to-date) for a short window. Several implementation approaches exist, ordered from simplest to most sophisticated.

The simplest is **time-based routing**: after any write, route that user's reads to the primary for the next 5 seconds. This covers most replication lag scenarios. The implementation stores the last-write timestamp per user in a fast store like Redis and checks it in the read path.

```java
public DataSource routeRead(String userId) {
    Long lastWrite = redis.get("last_write:" + userId);
    if (lastWrite != null && 
        System.currentTimeMillis() - lastWrite < 5000) {
        return primaryDataSource;           // recent write, read from primary
    }
    return replicaDataSource;               // safe to read from replica
}
```

The follow-up approach uses **GTID (Global Transaction ID)**: after a write, record the transaction ID and only route to replicas that have replicated past that GTID. This is more precise than time-based routing but requires database-specific support.

The quorum approach generalizes this: if you write to W replicas and read from R replicas, and **W + R > N** (total replicas), at least one read server will have the latest write. This is the consistency model Cassandra and DynamoDB use. The trade-off is higher read latency because you're reading from multiple replicas and taking the latest.
